    "product_id": "9dcaffcc-262a-4a99-94ed-f4b9091ad907",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 020502\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1002 PM PDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures and quiet conditions continue into \n  next week as high pressure dominates.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Wednesday.\n\nA ridge of high pressure over the southwestern United States will\nbe the main driver of weather through the middle of next week. \nThis pattern will maintain anomalously warm temperatures for early\nOctober, with highs around 10 degrees above normal each \nafternoon. The position of the ridge is not conducive for moisture\nadvection and precipitation is not expected over the next seven \ndays. A weak shortwave approaches the Pacific coast on early next \nweek, which may bring slightly breezier winds and knock \ntemperatures down a few degrees on Sunday and Monday before the \nridge reestablishes itself.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds \nremain light and follow typical, daily patterns. Chance of seeing \n10+ knots is 10% or less throughout the TAF period. Clear skies \nuntil high clouds move in Friday evening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal wind \npatterns expected in most locations throughout the TAF period. \nBreezy conditions are forecast in the western Mojave Desert and \nsouthern Great Basin, but gusts should remain around 20 knots or \nless and generally be from a southerly or easterly direction. Clear \nskies overnight with high clouds moving in Friday afternoon and \nevening.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
